+---
+node: record-store
+---
+
+# Decision: Concurrent Visit Edits
+
+## Context
+
+Twenty-four field workers share twelve tablets that can be offline for 48 hours, and workers sometimes correct the same visit independently after comparing field notes. The record store therefore receives edits that started from the same visit state and know nothing of each other, sometimes days apart. It has to say what the visit's current value is when that happens.
+
+The answer shapes more than this node. It fixes what a tablet must remember and send with each edit, what a coordinator is asked to review and how often, and which visits can enter the reviewed CSV the science partner receives every Friday. Tablet-sync and review-export are both sized around it.
+
+Two things are at stake if it is wrong. One is the record itself: a visit whose current value combines observations nobody recorded together can reach the partner looking like any other reviewed row. The other is the weekly rhythm: two coordinators resolve every question about submitted observations, and work they cannot clear before Friday keeps visits out of that week's export.
+
+Every option keeps all original submissions and never lets arrival order decide between workers. Those hold whatever is chosen and do not separate the options.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+No gate eliminates either option. The project rules out automated inference of scientific intent, but combining edits to different fields by fixed rules is not inference, and nothing requires every combined record to be approved by hand.
+
+Weighted criteria:
+
+- **Integrity of the current visit — major.** Whether a visit's current value is always something one person recorded or approved as a whole. Fields of a visit are not independent: a species identification and its cover estimate are one observation, and a note may qualify an estimate. The importance comes from the tenet *keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable* — a value is only explainable if someone stood behind it.
+- **Coordinator workload and export readiness — major.** How much review each approach generates for two coordinators, and how many visits are held out of a Friday export waiting for it. This carries the goals *make reviewable problems visible before the Friday export* and *support independent corrections to the same visit*.
+- **Logic the team must keep correct — moderate.** How much behaviour encodes knowledge of the visit's fields and must be revisited whenever the survey protocol or the fields change. The importance comes from the tenet *prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*.
+- **Explaining one exported value — moderate.** How much a coordinator must reconstruct to answer the partner's question "where did this number come from?" Same tenet as integrity, seen from the export side.
+- **Burden on the tablets — minor.** What each tablet stores per visit and sends per edit, on shared devices that queue two days of work.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+Every edit is a complete visit that names the revision the worker started from. An edit whose starting revision has already been superseded does not replace anything: it stands beside the other as a competing revision, and the visit has no current value until a coordinator selects one of them or supplies a replacement. The server decides that revisions compete from their starting points alone and never looks inside them.
+
+Example: from the same starting revision, one worker corrects an observer's name and another adds a missing species. Both complete visits are kept, the visit goes to review, and a coordinator produces the visit that carries both corrections.
+
+- Integrity of the current visit: **strong** — every current value is a visit one worker submitted whole, or one a coordinator selected or wrote.
+- Coordinator workload and export readiness: **weak** — every pair of independent corrections needs a coordinator, including corrections to unrelated fields, and the visit is out of the export until then.
+- Logic the team must keep correct: **strong** — the rule for competing does not mention any field, so adding or changing fields does not touch it.
+- Explaining one exported value: **strong** — the value comes from one revision with one author, or from one named coordinator's resolution.
+- Burden on the tablets: **strong** — one revision ID kept per visit; each edit sends the visit as it now stands.
+
+Trades coordinator time for a record in which nothing is combined without a person doing it.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+Every edit supplies the state the worker started from and the changes made to it. When two edits started from the same state, the server compares each against that state: changes to different fields are combined into a new current visit automatically, and only changes to the same field go to a coordinator. This needs a rule for each kind of field — replace for single values such as the observation date, set-like handling for the observer list, per-species handling for cover estimates, and something for free-text notes — and it records, field by field, which submission each current value came from.
+
+Example: the observer-name correction and the added species above combine with no review, and the visit is in Friday's export.
+
+- Integrity of the current visit: **weak** — a combined visit is one nobody recorded or approved as a whole. If one worker changes a species identification and another changes that species' cover estimate, the rules see two different fields and join them. Rules can be written for the relationships the team knows about; they cannot cover the ones it has not thought of, and a crossed relationship produces no signal.
+- Coordinator workload and export readiness: **strong** — review is limited to edits that touch the same field, and everything else is ready for export at once.
+- Logic the team must keep correct: **weak** — the merge rules are a standing body of logic about the survey's fields that must change with the protocol, and whose mistakes are silent.
+- Explaining one exported value: **partial** — field-level provenance does answer the question, but the answer for one row can involve several submissions and the rule that joined them.
+- Burden on the tablets: **partial** — each tablet keeps a snapshot of the starting state per visit and sends structured changes rather than the visit.
+
+Trades a standing risk of unnoticed scientific inconsistency, and rules that need upkeep, for a short review queue.
+
+## Selection
+
+Whole-visit revisions.
+
+The two major criteria point in opposite directions, so the decision is which failure the team would rather live with. A long review queue is visible, bounded, and recoverable: coordinators can see it, a held visit goes out the following Friday, and nothing wrong reaches the partner in the meantime. A wrongly combined visit is none of those. It carries no mark, it is exported as reviewed data, and finding it later means someone noticing an implausible observation by eye. Given the tenet that the path to an exported value stays inspectable, the visible cost is the one to accept. The moderate criteria agree: the team has no one whose job is to keep merge rules in step with the survey protocol.
+
+**What this forecloses.** Corrections to unrelated fields do not combine on their own. Every independent correction of the same visit costs coordinator time, and that visit misses any export that falls before it is resolved.
+
+**Why field-aware merge remains worth considering.** It lost on which failure is tolerable, not on merit: it is the only option that keeps review proportional to real disagreement. It should be reopened if any of these hold:
+
+- Visits are regularly held out of the Friday export because coordinators cannot clear the queue.
+- Revision history shows that most competing revisions touch different fields. The store keeps every revision with its starting point, so this can be measured from real seasons rather than guessed.
+- The team gains someone who can own merge rules as the protocol changes.
+
+Reopening it does not require starting over. A complete visit plus the revision it started from is everything a three-way comparison needs, so the stored history can be merged after the fact, and a narrower version — combining only field pairs the team has explicitly declared independent, or proposing a combined visit for a coordinator to approve — could be adopted without changing what tablets send.
+
+**What tablet-sync must accommodate.**
+
+- Keep, for every visit held on the tablet, the ID of the revision the local copy came from, and send it with the next edit.
+- Send the whole visit on every edit.
+- Submit a worker's successive offline edits to one visit in order, because each starts from the revision ID the server returned for the one before.
+- Tell the worker when a submitted edit is competing rather than current, and with which other revisions. "Accepted" does not mean "this is now the record."
+- Expect a visit the tablet believes is settled to be in review, or settled differently, when it next connects.
+
+**What review-export must accommodate.**
+
+- A comparison screen that shows competing complete visits side by side against their common starting point. The server does not say which fields differ; the screen works that out for display.
+- A resolution step in which a coordinator either selects one competing revision or enters a replacement visit, naming exactly the revisions they were shown, and a path back to the screen when another revision arrived meanwhile.
+- A CSV that includes only settled visits. Visits in review are held out, so coordinators need to see what is being held before Friday.
+- A queue sized for every independent correction, not only for genuine disagreements.
